"The surah begins with an oath by the morning brightness and by the night when it grows still. Duha is the moment when the sun rises and begins to shine brightly—the youth of the day. Night, on the other hand, is the time when it fully settles, becomes calm, and is cloaked in silence. These two contrasting states recall the fluctuations of life, such as joy and sorrow.\n\nO Muhammad! Your Lord has not forsaken you, nor has He become displeased. The pause in revelation was not an abandonment, but a moment of rest necessitated by mercy and wisdom. Just as in the stillness of the night, this interval is a source of tranquility and a herald of forthcoming joys.\n\nFor you, the end of every state is better than its beginning. The life of prophethood, the arrival of revelation, its pause, and its resumption; the life of this world and the Hereafter—at every stage, there is a continuous ascent. You will advance from good to greater good day by day.\n\nYour Lord will bestow such favors upon you that you will be well-pleased. This promise also encompasses the station of intercession. The Prophet will intercede for his community and, by Allah's leave, satisfy them.\n\nDid He not find you an orphan and give you shelter? The Prophet was born fatherless and also lost his mother and grandfather. Allah protected him under the care of his uncle Abu Talib and reared him in an incomparable manner.\n\nDid He not find you unaware of the way and guide you? The Prophet was far removed from polytheism, yet before divine revelation arrived, he did not know the details of the true religion. Allah showed him the right path by granting him the Book and wisdom.\n\nDid He not find you poor and make you rich? While materially poor, he became enriched through blessings in trade and the wealth of Khadijah. Spiritually as well, he brought richness to his community.\n\nTherefore, never oppress the orphan, nor look down upon him considering him weak. Protect his rights and treat him with compassion. Do not chide the petitioner, whether asking for material aid or for knowledge—help him to the best of your ability or turn him away with gentle words.\n\nProclaim the blessing of your Lord. Give thanks through word and deed, and let others benefit as well. Recognize the value of these blessings and spread them.\n\nReciting the takbir at the end of this surah is Sunnah. It has been narrated from the Prophet that 'Allahu Akbar' is said at the conclusion of each surah. This practice is an expression of joy following the pause in revelation.\n\nAt the conclusion of this blessed surah, following the sorrow felt over the interruption of revelation, glad tidings are given that with the relief brought by the brilliance of the morning sun and the continuation of the Lord's help, the end is better than the beginning. Ultimately, by awakening feelings of compassion for the orphan and mercy for the needy, the surah concludes with the command to recount the blessing one possesses. It conveys what a beautiful compliance with the command, what a delightful declaration of peace of heart, and what a profound sense of gratitude it expresses to hasten to proclaim this blessing with complete faith and inner tranquility by pronouncing the takbir, 'Allahu Akbar'.\n\nTo avoid giving the impression that this takbir is part of the Quran, and to indicate that it is not obligatory but left to the sentiments of those who experience that spiritual delight and meaning, it was not transmitted through successive narration (tawatur) in the recitations; rather, it was narrated as a specific practice and, in one respect, a command of the Messenger of Allah, such that omitting it is permissible. As a result, it has come to be practiced customarily as recommended (mandub) and desirable (mustahabb).\n\nIn current practice, it is mostly said as 'Allahu Akbar'; frequently, it is also recited as 'Allahu Akbar, la ilaha illallahu wallahu akbar wa lillahi'l-hamd'. For extensive information on the subject, one should consult al-Jazari's 'an-Nashr al-Kabir'.\n\nUpon this relief and peace of heart that dispels sorrows, one should not fall into indolence under the presumption that no hardship or trouble remains in the world. Knowing that along with every hardship there is ease, one must strive even harder to proclaim the blessing possessed. The importance is emphasized not merely of seeing the blessing, but of looking to the Essence of the One who bestows that blessing, directing all longing toward Him, and striving to journey toward Him.\n\nFor this reason, this surah is followed by Surah al-Inshirah. Surah al-Inshirah reinforces the same theme, teaching that the believer must turn to Allah and give thanks in every circumstance."